          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The dragon’s primary symbolic identity is Satan, the personal power of evil who persecutes God’s people and empowers hostile political powers; rulers, empires, and chaos-monster imagery can function as related manifestations or backgrounds.\",\"book_summary\":\"The dragon is the Devil and Satan understood as the devouring spiritual power operating behind earthly beasts and kingdoms and granting authority to the beast.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The dragon is Satan, the Devil.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The dragon is Satan, the Devil, acting as the spiritual power behind hostile kingdoms and beasts.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement is established. The book foregrounds Satan’s agency behind political powers, but that relationship is already recognized in the consensus. Its omission of other dragon imagery does not by itself reject those contextual senses.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. Both identify the dragon centrally as Satan and connect him with oppressive earthly powers.\",\"rationale\":\"After reducing both readings to their minimum identification, both say that the dragon is Satan. The book’s description of Satan as empowering beasts and kingdoms is a role specification already present in the consensus, especially through Revelation 13, rather than a replaced referent or material change in scope.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Revelation 12:9 and 13:2 strongly establish that the dragon is Satan and empowers the beast, but Ezekiel 29:3 and Jeremiah 51:34 directly apply dragon imagery to Pharaoh and Nebuchadnezzar; they do not by themselves prove that a distinct spiritual power stands behind those rulers. That broader synthesis is inferential.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
